// THREE ROWS IN A COLUMN THE CALL SITE ALREADY DRAWS.
// `.selected__player-header-item` (selected-player.styl) is a centring flex
// column, so the label, the price and the footnote each take their own line and
// each centres on the item's width without a rule here. That is the whole
// reason this file no longer carries the `1fr auto 1fr` grid it did while the
// footnote sat BESIDE the price: side by side, the two centred as a unit and
// the price drifted left as the footnote changed width, so the price had to be
// pinned to the middle track of a grid whose empty first track cost as much
// width as the footnote itself -- 164px at `$45 from $33` against 96px bare.
// Stacked, the centring is structural and the width is back to the label's.
//
// The label's own size, weight and casing are NOT here either, for the same
// reason: both call sites style `label` inside their own header rows, and this
// component is a value in someone else's row rather than a row of its own.

// THE LABEL CARRIES THE ICON, so it stops being a line of text and becomes a
// row of two things.
//
// QUALIFIED BY THE COMPONENT ON PURPOSE. Both call sites set `display` on a
// bare `label` inside a class -- `.selected__player-header label` and
// `.nominated__player-details label` -- and those rank 0,1,1. A rule written
// `.player__auction-value label` ranks 0,1,1 too, which is a TIE decided by
// chunk order rather than intent, and the drawer's `display flex` would have
// masked the loss on one surface while the bar's `display block` broke the row
// on the other. Naming the label's own class lifts this to 0,2,0 so it wins
// outright on both.
.player__auction-value .player__auction-value-label
  display flex
  flex-direction row
  align-items center
  justify-content center
  gap 3px

// 12px, AND IT HAS TO BEAT `.icon`. icon.styl sizes every glyph 24px with an
// 18px `small` variant, both far too big beside a 9-to-10px label -- and both
// are ordinary CSS rather than the inline style PlayerHeadshot writes, so a
// rule CAN win here. `.icon.player__auction-value-info` doubles up to 0,2,0
// because the single-class form ties `.icon` at 0,1,0 and would be settled by
// whichever chunk webpack emitted last.
//
// `fill currentColor` on `.icon` means the glyph takes the label's own ink, so
// it reads as part of the label rather than as a control sitting next to it.
// `flex 0 0 auto` because the label is a flex row now and a shrunk circle looks
// like a rendering fault rather than a smaller icon.
.icon.player__auction-value-info
  width 12px
  height 12px
  flex 0 0 auto
  opacity 0.55

// SMALLER THAN THE LABEL ABOVE IT, which is the point of putting it on its own
// line. `em` rather than a fixed size, so it tracks whatever the surrounding
// row set for the price -- the drawer and the auction bar both render the price
// at 18px, but neither promises to, and a footnote pinned at 9px would stop
// being a footnote the moment one of them changed.
//
// 0.5, not 0.55. Against 18px the two are 9px and 9.9px, and the drawer's own
// label is 9px -- so 0.55 made the footnote fractionally LARGER than the label
// it is subordinate to on one of the two surfaces, which is the hierarchy this
// rule exists to state. 0.5 is at or under the label on both.
//
// `nowrap`, because `+$12 from $33` broken across two lines reads as two
// separate annotations and doubles the item's height in a bar whose band is
// 70px.
//
// COLOUR CARRIES THE DIRECTION and the copy carries the magnitude.
// $successColor for over-market and a lightened $textColor for under. NOT red
// for under: red is $accentColor, and the auction bar spends it on the standing
// election a few inches away, so an under-market footnote in red would read as
// an alert about the bid rather than as a direction.
.player__auction-value-context
  font-size 0.5em
  font-weight 500
  line-height 1.2
  white-space nowrap
  padding-top 1px
  &.over
    color $successColor
  &.under
    color lighten($textColor, 25)
